      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "International organizations like the UN often fail to protect specific nations because their consensus-based decision-making requires all major powers to agree, effectively vetoing any action that challenges the interests of geopolitically dominant states. This structural design prioritizes diplomatic stability over the urgent defense of individual sovereignty, allowing powerful blocs to co-opt or dilute the mandates of less influential nations. Consequently, these global bodies frequently serve as platforms for hegemony rather than as genuine guardians of state autonomy.",
